> Define closure over a finite chart

An item in column k has coordinates (production id, dot, origin).
For A→α.β with origin j, α has matched tokens in [j,k), and β
remains. A symbol is nonterminal exactly when it occurs as a production
left-hand side. The operations are:

- Prediction: before a nonterminal B, add each original B→.γ at origin k.
- Scanning: before a matching terminal token, advance the dot into column k+1.
- Completion: a finished B from j to k advances every item in column j waiting for B.

Seed every original start production in column zero. Acceptance requires a
completed start production at the final boundary, with origin zero. No
synthetic start item is added, preserving the original chart statistics.
Rules retain their original ids and duplicate rules remain distinct items.

Soundness follows by induction over these deductions: prediction starts an
empty prefix, scanning consumes the specified token, and completion joins
adjacent matching spans. For completeness, every successful derivation's
prediction, token advance and completed child must eventually be joined.
An agenda processes each inserted item once, and deduplication makes its
closure finite. If M is the sum of RHS-length-plus-one over all original
productions, at most M(N+1)(N+2)/2 distinct items can occur across N+1
columns: each dotted production has at most k+1 origins in column k.
This remains finite even when a nullable cycle admits infinitely many trees.

The implementation interns opaque symbol strings, predicts a requested
nonterminal once per column, and indexes waiters and first completed spans.
It uses integer comparisons in the recognition loop. Insertion-ordered
vectors determine output order; hash indexes only answer membership/lookup
queries. Expected constant hash access gives the usual fixed-grammar
O(N³) general join bound and O(N²) item/index storage. String preparation
is additional. These are not unconditional worst-case hash-time claims.
No lookahead, LR(0) precomputation, Leo optimization or parse forest is included.

> Retain the original balanced words and left-recursive expressions

The original grammar S→a S b | a b recognizes a^k b^k for k>=1.
The next program retains all six original tests. It also retains the
left-recursive precedence grammar E→E+T|T, T→T*F|F, F→(E)|id
and its three original input words. Naive recursive descent on that
left recursion would call itself without consuming a token; Earley needs
no grammar rewrite. The E/T/F grammar specifies precedence and is not
the ambiguous S→SS|a grammar used later.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/earley_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::ContextFreeGrammar balanced{{{"S",{"a","S","b"}},{"S",{"a","b"}}},"S"};
  std::print("| balanced word | accepted |\n| :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& word:std::vector<std::vector<std::string>>{{"a","b"},{"a","a","b","b"},{"a","a","a","b","b","b"},{"a","a","b"},{"a","b","b"},{"b","a"}}){
    std::string text;for(const auto& token:word)text+=token;std::print("| {} | {} |\n",text,dm::algorithms::earley_recognize(balanced,word));}
  dm::algorithms::ContextFreeGrammar expr{{{"E",{"E","+","T"}},{"E",{"T"}},{"T",{"T","*","F"}},{"T",{"F"}},{"F",{"(","E",")"}},{"F",{"id"}}},"E"};
  std::print("\n| expression tokens | accepted |\n| :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& word:std::vector<std::vector<std::string>>{{"id","+","id","*","id"},{"(","id","+","id",")","*","id"},{"id","+"}}){
    std::string text;for(const auto& token:word){if(!text.empty())text+=' ';text+=token;}std::print("| {} | {} |\n",text,dm::algorithms::earley_recognize(expr,word));}
  const std::vector<std::string> big{"a","a","a","a","b","b","b","b"};
  const auto sizes=dm::algorithms::earley_chart_sizes(balanced,big);const auto t=dm::algorithms::earley_trace(balanced,big);
  std::print("\nOriginal aaaabbbb chart sizes:");for(int s:sizes)std::print(" {}",s);std::print(". Total items {}; capture entries {}.\n",t.work.unique_items,t.captured_entries);
  std::vector<double> ys(sizes.begin(),sizes.end());std::vector<std::string> labels;for(std::size_t i=0;i<sizes.size();++i)labels.push_back(std::to_string(i));
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(ys,labels,"",{16,122,87}).title("Original chart for aaaabbbb").x_label("input boundary").y_label("unique dotted items")
   .y_limits(0,5.5).size(1100,500).save_svg("build/earley_original.svg");
}
