> What was verified

Separate old/current binaries preserve 8,194 original verdict/chart-size
cases: every a/b word through length twelve and all original expression
examples. The old nullable use-after-free is reproduced separately; the
new seven-case sanitizer suite covers all 8,191 short a/b words, 7,560
queries on seeded nullable/cyclic grammars against an independent least
fixed point of interval languages, rule-order and duplicate controls,
opaque symbols, exact/short/disabled capture limits, ownership and a
20,000-rule unit chain without recursive library calls.

Every captured item's first parents are checked as a sound earlier
prediction, scan or completion. Final-chart certificates independently
count requested predictions, matching scans, distinct completed spans
and all waiter/span pairs, verifying work identities without replaying
the implementation. A finite item chart establishes recognition, not a
count or enumeration of all parses. A rejected word also does not provide
automatic error recovery or an explanation of the intended source text.

> References

J. Earley (1970), [An Efficient Context-Free Parsing Algorithm](https://doi.org/10.1145/362007.362035),
Communications of the ACM 13(2), 94–102, introduces the chart algorithm.

J. Aycock and R. N. Horspool (2002), [Practical Earley Parsing](https://webhome.cs.uvic.ca/~nigelh/Publications/PracticalEarleyParsing.pdf),
The Computer Journal 45(6), 620–630, doi:10.1093/comjnl/45.6.620,
explains the nullable late-waiter problem and reviews Earley's earlier
completed-symbol remedy. The implementation here uses indexed waiters
and completed spans for that handshake; it does not implement their
LR(0) automaton construction or claim to return a parse forest.
